  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>An EICR code is not an opinion. C1, C2, C3 and FI have set meanings. For rented homes in England the government guidance defines them. Your code decides if an installation is safe. Get it wrong and a danger stays live, or a landlord misses the 28-day deadline.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>C1 means danger now. Remedial work is required, and the EICR is Unsatisfactory.</li><li>C2 means potentially dangerous. The EICR is Unsatisfactory.</li><li>C3 means improvement recommended. It does not make the EICR Unsatisfactory.</li><li>FI means further investigation is needed without delay. In a rented home the landlord must arrange it. Industry practice is to treat the EICR as Unsatisfactory until it is resolved.</li><li>In England, a landlord must fix C1 and C2 faults within 28 days, or sooner if the report says so.</li><li>Quickler shows C1 and C2 on the dashboard as you enter them. You choose the codes.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The codes",
      "heading": "Four codes, one outcome",
      "html": "<p>An EICR uses a small set of fixed codes. They come from industry guidance used with BS 7671, and for rented homes in England the government guidance sets them out. They are not opinion.</p><p>You cannot make a C2 into a C3 because the client seems relaxed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "C1",
      "heading": "Danger now",
      "html": "<p>C1 means danger is present and someone could be hurt now.</p><p>Examples: bare live conductors, missing protective devices, damaged wiring with bare cores, no earthing where it is needed.</p><p>Tell the client in writing, and advise against using the affected part until it is fixed and checked (common practice). If you can make it safe on the spot, do. If you cannot, make the danger clear before you leave.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "C2",
      "heading": "Potentially dangerous",
      "html": "<p>C2 means the fault could become dangerous. It may get worse, or someone untrained may disturb it.</p><p>Examples: poor protection from damage, no RCD where one is now needed, wrong cable sizes, old wiring methods.</p><p>The EICR is Unsatisfactory. Work is needed. In a rental, the landlord must do it within 28 days, or sooner if the report says so.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "C3 and FI",
      "heading": "Improve, or look closer",
      "html": "<p>C3 means an improvement is recommended. The installation is not dangerous. A report with only C3 codes is Satisfactory. The client decides.</p><p>FI means further investigation. Use it when you cannot judge part of the installation, such as hidden wiring. The landlord must arrange the further investigation. Industry practice is to treat the EICR as Unsatisfactory until it is resolved.</p><p>Do not use FI to avoid a hard call. Say what must be checked and what access you need.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The law",
      "heading": "Timescales and what the report says",
      "html": "<p>The report must give the date of the next inspection. The interval is the inspector's recommendation. Common practice (IET guidance, not law) is up to 10 years for an owner-occupied home and 5 years for a business. For a private rented home in England the limit is 5 years, or earlier if the report says so. If there are C1 or C2 faults, say how soon they must be fixed.</p><p>In England, a private landlord must fix the faults within 28 days of an Unsatisfactory EICR, or sooner if the report says so. They then get written confirmation from the qualified person who did the works, and give it to the tenant and the local council within 28 days of completion.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "On site",
      "heading": "See C1 and C2 as you find them",
      "html": "<p>You answer in WhatsApp. No new app to install. No login on site.</p><p>When you enter a C1 or C2, it shows on the dashboard at once. The office does not wait for you to get back.</p><p>See <a href=\"/pages/eicr-software-uk.html\">EICR software for electricians</a>, the <a href=\"/pages/eicr-checklist-uk.html\">EICR checklist</a> and <a href=\"/pages/eicr-rental-property-uk.html\">EICR for rental properties</a>.</p>"
    },
