      "html": "<h3>What does C1 mean on an EICR?</h3><p>Danger is present and there is a risk of injury. Remedial work is required, and the EICR is Unsatisfactory. Tell the client at once, in writing (common practice).</p><h3>What is the difference between C2 and C3?</h3><p>C2 is potentially dangerous. It makes the EICR Unsatisfactory. C3 is an improvement. It does not.</p><h3>What does FI mean on an EICR?</h3><p>Further investigation. You use it when you cannot judge part of the installation. The landlord must arrange the further investigation. Industry practice is to treat the EICR as Unsatisfactory until it is resolved.</p><h3>How long does a landlord have to fix a C2?</h3><p>In England, 28 days from the Unsatisfactory EICR, or sooner if the report says so. A C1 needs action at once.</p>"
